# LSan suppression file — CPython by-design teardown leaks (Step 0.4.1).
#
# ROLE: leak-AUDIT mode ONLY (sanitizer_runtime_env(leak_audit=True)). NOT part of
# the feasibility gate — operational runs use detect_leaks=0 (see SANITIZER_POLICY.md
# for why; evidence logs/env/STEP_0.4.1_sanitizer_probe.log sections A/F/H).
#
# Each entry suppresses leaks whose allocation stack passes through a CPython
# allocator that does not free at interpreter teardown by design. Justification is
# per line; the allocators were observed in probe section A (interning + import).
# Known limitation (probe H): these function-level patterns leave a small residual
# (~699 B / 56 allocs) and cannot perfectly separate CPython from candidate leaks,
# which is exactly why leak detection is OFF operationally.

# Interned str / unicode objects built during import & kept for process lifetime
# (PyUnicode_FromKindAndData in probe section A). By-design: CPython never frees
# the interned-string table at exit.
leak:PyUnicode_
# Small-object arena allocations for interned/cached objects (PyObject_Malloc top
# frame of the section-A leak stacks). By-design: pymalloc arenas live until exit.
leak:PyObject_Malloc
# Raw object-memory allocations on the same teardown-immortal paths.
leak:PyMem_
# GC-tracked container objects (type dicts, module dicts) created at import and
# never collected because the interpreter holds the last reference at exit.
leak:_PyObject_GC
